---
/**
 * The glossary every ⓘ mark on the site links into.
 *
 * Entry ids MUST match the keys of STRINGS.infoTip.defs — the InfoTip component builds its
 * href from the key, so a renamed id here silently strands every mark that pointed at it.
 * Additional entries (terms with no ⓘ yet) are fine; missing ones are not.
 *
 * Prose stays in this file per the strings-module policy: these sentences interleave links
 * and emphasis, and splitting them into string fragments would make them untranslatable.
 */
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import { STRINGS } from '../constants/strings';
import * as route from '../data/routes';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title={STRINGS.glossaryPage.title}
  description={STRINGS.glossaryPage.description}
  section="glossary"
>
  <div class="prose-shell">
    <PageHeader
      eyebrow={STRINGS.glossaryPage.eyebrow}
      title={STRINGS.glossaryPage.heading}
      standfirst={STRINGS.glossaryPage.standfirst}
    />

    <div class="prose-page">
      <h2 id="the-cfr">The CFR</h2>
      <p>
        The <strong>Code of Federal Regulations</strong> is the official rulebook of the United
        States federal government — every general and permanent rule its agencies have issued,
        organised into 50 numbered <em>titles</em> by subject (Title 21 is food and drugs, Title
        40 is the environment, and so on). Laws passed by Congress say <em>what</em> must happen;
        the CFR is where agencies spell out <em>how</em>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="the-ecfr">The eCFR</h2>
      <p>
        The <a href="https://www.ecfr.gov" rel="external noopener">electronic CFR</a> is the
        continuously updated online edition of the CFR, published by the Office of the Federal
        Register and the Government Publishing Office. It is updated within days of rule changes
        — and it is the source this site measures. Note that the eCFR itself is an editorial
        compilation, not the legally authoritative edition; see
        <a href={route.about()}>about &amp; attribution</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="titles-chapters-parts">Titles, chapters, parts, sections</h2>
      <p>
        The rulebook nests like a book: a <strong>title</strong> covers a broad subject, a
        <strong>chapter</strong> within it usually belongs to one agency, a <strong>part</strong>
        covers one program or topic, and a <strong>section</strong> is the individual rule you
        cite — “40 CFR 60.290” means title 40, part 60, section 60.290.
      </p>

      <h2 id="total-regulation">Total regulation</h2>
      <p>
        An agency's <strong>total regulation</strong> is every rule it manages, measured in
        words. Where a rule is managed jointly with another agency, it counts <em>in full</em>
        here — if two agencies are each fully responsible for a rule, each one's total should
        reflect that. Because of this, adding every agency's total together counts jointly
        managed rules more than once; use <a href="#share-of-regulation">shares</a> for sums.
      </p>

      <h2 id="share-of-regulation">Share of all regulation</h2>
      <p>
        An agency's <strong>share</strong> is its slice of the whole rulebook: jointly managed
        rules are split evenly between the agencies in charge, so every word lands in exactly
        one agency's share and shares can be fairly compared or added. When an agency manages
        nothing jointly, its share and its total are the same number.
      </p>

      <h2 id="total-federal-regulation">Total federal regulation</h2>
      <p>
        The size of the whole rulebook in words, counting every rule once. This is the site's
        headline number, and the honest answer to “how much federal regulation is there?”
      </p>

      <h2 id="counting-shared-in-full">Counting shared rules once per agency</h2>
      <p>
        The alternative corpus figure: the same rulebook, but a rule managed by several agencies
        is counted once for <em>each</em> of them. It answers “how much regulation are agencies
        responsible for, in total?” — a responsibility measure, not a size measure, which is why
        it runs slightly higher than the total above.
      </p>

      <h2 id="shared-rules">Shared rules (joint management)</h2>
      <p>
        Some rules have more than one agency in charge — the EPA and the Department of Justice
        share one chapter of environmental rules, for example. The eCFR names every agency for
        each of these. <a href={route.sharedJurisdiction()}>See every jointly managed area →</a>
      </p>

      <h2 id="areas-managed">Areas managed (scopes)</h2>
      <p>
        The eCFR assigns each agency named slices of the rulebook — a whole title, a chapter, or
        a single part. This site calls them <em>areas</em> (the data calls them
        <em>scopes</em>). An agency's numbers are built by measuring each of its areas.
      </p>

      <h2 id="coverage">Coverage</h2>
      <p>
        How many of an agency's areas could actually be measured in the current build. When even
        one area cannot be measured, the agency's totals are reported as unmeasured rather than
        as a partial sum — a partial sum looks exactly like a real number, and this site never
        publishes one.
      </p>

      <h2 id="word-counts">Word counts</h2>
      <p>
        Every count is produced by parsing the eCFR's own XML and counting the words of actual
        regulation text — headings, authority notes, source citations and tables of contents are
        excluded (measured at 18.4% of the text in a sample chapter). Nothing is estimated from
        file sizes. The full rules are on the
        <a href={route.methodology()}>methodology page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="reserved">Reserved</h2>
      <p>
        A slot the rulebook keeps but that contains no rules — a placeholder for future use or
        the site of a repealed rule. Reserved items show a word count of zero, which is a
        measured fact, not a missing value.
      </p>

      <h2 id="not-measured">“Not measured” and the em dash (—)</h2>
      <p>
        When a value could not be measured, this site shows an em dash with the reason — never a
        zero, and never a guess. Every unmeasured value in the build is listed on the
        <a href={route.dataQuality()}>data quality page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="amendments">Amendments</h2>
      <p>
        A change to a rule, published by the issuing agency. The amendment charts count sections
        changed per month, dated by the day the change was published in the eCFR.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
